$$
\begin{aligned}
\mathrm{d}F(X(t),t) &= \frac{\partial F}{\partial t}\,\mathrm{d}t + \frac{\partial F}{\partial x}\,\mathrm{d}X(t)
\end{aligned}
$$

is inherently defined by an SIS. This relationship is reciprocal: the equation itself is equivalent to the condition

$$
\begin{aligned}
\mathrm{d}F(X(t),t) &= \frac{\partial F}{\partial t}\,\mathrm{d}t + \frac{\partial F}{\partial x}\,\mathrm{d}X(t) + \frac{1}{2}\frac{\partial^2 F}{\partial x^2}\,\mathrm{d}X(t)^2
\end{aligned}
$$

where the term $\mathrm{d}X(t)^2$ is defined as the quadratic variation of $X(t)$. This equivalence establishes a rigorous link between stochastic integrators and Itô calculus, which is essential for the analysis of stochastic differential equations.

